Verdict

MAJOR REVISION.

The central point-count formulas survive: Prop X0-2 is correct under its stated hypotheses; the F_{ℓ²} identity and coefficient (ℓ−1)/12 are correct; Prop X1 is also correct with all its hypotheses retained.

However, the draft contains a false experimental claim, an already-solved “OPEN” problem, false versions of Prop AGG-H, unjustified extensions of Prop CAT, inconsistent class-number tiers, and several incorrect summaries. The supersingular explanation also needs substantial prior-art attribution.

Locations below refer to geometry_results.md v1.1.

Issues

1. MAJOR — E3 did not recover every unknown symbol.

Location: row 16; §3.2, lines 297–299; OPEN-1.

A counterexample occurs inside the registered sample:
N = 3,321,346,453 = 52,489 × 63,277, the 32-bit seed 6.

The counts at ℓ = 2,3,5,7,11,13 are respectively:
6, 8, 16, 20, 16, 40.

decode_eps_from_x0_counts returns ε = 1 and ncons = 2. The two consistent patterns differ at precisely these entries:

    (χ_{−40}(p), χ_{−35}(p)) = (−1,+1)
    (χ_{−40}(p), χ_{−35}(p)) = (+1,−1).

Thus the claim “every unknown symbol … 4000/4000” is false. The code computes ncons but never checks or records uniqueness: run_experiments.py, lines 126–144.

Fix: report recovery of ε and consistency of the true pattern, which are what E3 actually measures. Record the distribution of ncons before making any stronger claim.

2. MAJOR — OPEN-1 is already solved as stated.

Location: §8, lines 500 onward; §0, lines 85–87.

The stated task is to factor in time subexponential in n = log N, given N and some point counts. Ignore the point counts and use an existing subexponential factoring algorithm. This is unconditional in expected time; it does not require the NFS heuristic. [Lenstra–Pomerance’s theorem](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf) supplies such an algorithm.

The asserted equivalence with all small fundamental-discriminant symbols is also unproved and stronger than the trace formula. At L = 13, the discriminants actually appearing are:

    −3, −4, −7, −8, −11, −19, −20, −24,
    −35, −40, −43, −51, −52.

For example, −23, −31 and −47 do not appear. Moreover, issue 1 disproves the claimed experimental uniqueness even for the symbols that do appear.

Fix: ask for polynomial time, or specify an improvement over a precise existing factoring bound. Replace “equivalent” by the proved reduction from counts to a specified collection of residue indicators. Label the information-theoretic reconstruction discussion heuristic.

3. MAJOR — Prop AGG-H gives the wrong period for mapping tori.

Location: §5, lines 412–418; row 24.

Let A = [[2,1],[1,1]], and take the torus mapping torus of A^N. For G = C₂,

    #Hom(π₁(M_{A^N}), C₂)
      = 2 · |ker(A^N−I on F₂²)|.

A mod 2 has order 3. Consequently the count is 8 when 3 divides N and 2 otherwise. In particular:

    N = 15: count 8;
    N = 35: count 2.

Both levels are odd semiprimes and congruent modulo exp(C₂) = 2.

Fix: distinguish literal exponent relators from iterated automorphisms. For a fixed mapping torus and fixed target, use the order of the induced permutation on the finite representation set. The aggregate conclusion survives, but the asserted modulus and proof do not.

4. MAJOR — Twisted Dijkgraaf–Witten invariants do not follow “hence” from homomorphism counts.

Location: the same proposition, lines 415–417.

The cocycle weights contain information about the fundamental class that is absent from the presentation’s solution set.

For the nontrivial cyclic C₃ theory on L(N,1), when 3 divides N, the standard normalization gives

    Z(L(N,1)) = (1/3) Σ_{a=0}² exp(2πi N a²/9).

The values at N = 15 and N = 21 differ, although both are 0 modulo exp(C₃) = 3. Orientation conventions can conjugate both values without removing the counterexample. The cyclic cocycles and lens-space evaluation are given in [de Wild Propitius, §2.3 and §2.9](https://staff.science.uva.nl/f.a.bais/scripties/mdwp1995.pdf).

Fix: restrict the homomorphism-count argument to untwisted DW theory. Prove any twisted periodicity statement separately, allowing a larger period.

5. MAJOR — Prop CAT does not prove factoring equivalence for a fixed knot.

Location: row 26; §4.2, lines 393–397.

The proved reduction changes the trace to t = 2+R as a function of N. It proves a result about a variable toral automorphism.

This cannot simply be interpreted as a family of genus-one fibred knots in S³. Their Alexander polynomial satisfies Δ(1) = ±1. For characteristic polynomial x²−tx+1, this requires 2−t = ±1; with t ≥ 3, only t = 3 remains.

The fixed figure-eight experiment is not a proof of factoring equivalence.

Fix: retain the theorem for the variable-trace toral family. Label the fixed figure-eight and fixed-knot claims OPEN or experimentally supported.

6. MAJOR — Additive and multiplicative Möbius inversion are confused.

Location: §5, lines 427–429.

The cyclotomic factor

    |Res(Δ, Φ_N)|

is obtained multiplicatively from the cover-homology orders. Prop CAT instead takes an additive Möbius sum.

For the figure-eight monodromy, write F_d = |det(A^d−I)|. Direct computation gives

    F₁ = 1, F₃ = 16, F₅ = 121, F₁₅ = 1,860,496.

At N = 15:

    additive primitive-point count = 1,860,360;
    multiplicative cyclotomic factor = 961.

They are not “exactly” the same parts.

Fix: write the two inversion formulas separately and remove the asserted identification.

7. MAJOR — The integer homology-size claim needs hypotheses.

Location: row 25; §5, lines 427–429.

H₁ of a cyclic branched cover need not be finite. For the trefoil, Δ(t)=t²−t+1 vanishes at sixth roots of unity, and the sixfold branched cover has positive first Betti number.

Nor are the finite orders always exponentially long. The trefoil has Mahler measure 1; its finite homology orders are periodic.

Fix: distinguish |H₁| from |H₁,tors|, state the resultant’s nonvanishing condition, and require positive logarithmic Mahler measure for exponential digit growth. [Silver–Williams](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0040-9383(01)00014-3) explicitly distinguish these cases.

8. MAJOR — The class-number FACT claims contradict the draft’s own caveat.

Location: rows 5, 11, 19; §3.2, lines 338–340; §3.3, lines 346–347.

Row 5 cautiously calls extraction from one class number heuristic. The Fricke paragraph then says “hence FACT,” and §3.3 repeats unconditional factoring equivalence.

A class number, an explicitly supplied class-group structure, and a splitting ambiguous form are different outputs. A failed simple powering argument neither establishes nor disproves a more sophisticated reduction.

The reverse direction also needs attention: knowing the factorization of N does not provide a known polynomial-time computation of h(−4N). Thus “equivalent” requires more than a proposed factor extractor.

Fix: use a separate class-number status consistently, specifying conditional reductions and sampling/generation assumptions. Do not infer FACT merely from the occurrence of a class number.

9. MAJOR — The random-walk QR classification omits identity and scalar reductions.

Location: row 20; §7, random walks.

The formula

    #Fix(W on P¹(F_r)) = 1 + (disc(W)/r)

requires W mod r to be nonscalar.

Take W = [[5,3],[3,2]], the square of the trace-3 cat map. It is hyperbolic, but W mod 3 = −I. At N = 15, explicit enumeration gives four fixed points; the displayed product formula gives one.

In addition, unrestricted return-word sums include identity words, whose fixed-point contribution is ψ(N). Words of growing length can also have discriminants far beyond “small fixed D.”

Fix: handle scalar reductions and identity words explicitly. State precisely which words, discriminant sizes and exceptional level primes the QR conclusion permits.

10. MAJOR — Several “general principles” exceed the propositions proved.

Location: §0, lines 75–83; §4, lines 399–401.

“Divisor sums of non-monomial weights are factoring-equivalent” is false without substantial restrictions. The classical identity Σ_{d|N} φ(d)=N is an immediate counterexample to the broad wording.

“Periods are orders, hence factoring-equivalent” also needs the relevant oracle and randomization assumptions; the usual reduction is not a theorem about every fixed-base period.

Fix: replace these assertions by the actual quantified statements of MF, NECK and CAT. Make the distinction between structural classification and a proved complexity reduction explicit.

11. MINOR — The supersingular reading identifies the wrong trace-formula term.

Location: §3.2, lines 314–320.

A1 = ψ/12 is the square-index identity term. It is not itself the supersingular locus.

For example, N = 221 and ℓ = 2 give A1 = 21, whereas the supersingular contribution over F₄ is 22. The latter includes automorphism-orbit corrections.

The coefficient (ℓ−1)/12 in the final point count is correct; the attribution of the entire locus to A1 is not.

Fix: call A1 the identity contribution. Derive the supersingular count separately using the mass formula and automorphism orbits.

12. MINOR — The “at most six symbols” bound is not uniform in ℓ.

Location: §3.2, lines 307–309.

The code’s discriminant lists, including −4 and −3, have sizes:

    ℓ = 2,3,5,7,11: 4,6,8,11,22.

The six-symbol bound concerns the tested F₄/F₉ decoders. Also, “bounded QR terms” means bounded in N for fixed ℓ and a fixed semiprime promise; it is not a uniform bound over arbitrary squarefree N and growing ℓ.

Fix: state those parameter dependencies explicitly.

13. MINOR — Prop X0-1 and its decoder omit the prime 3.

Location: §3.1, lines 231–235; decoders.py, lines 64–70.

For N = 39 = 3×13:

    ν₂ = 0, ν₃ = 2, ψ = 56, genus = 3.

The assertion ν₃ ∈ {0,4} is false, and from_genus(39,3) returns None.

Fix: remove the factor 3 first or include the exceptional elliptic-point value. In the preceding “any N” formulas, use the appropriate Kronecker symbol at 2; the code’s −4 is safer than the text’s −1.

14. MINOR — The CW face bound is false, although the size obstruction survives.

Location: row 12, versus §3.1.

A closed genus-g surface has a CW structure with one vertex, 2g edges and one face. Therefore arbitrary CW structures do not require ψ/6−O(1) faces.

The lower bound on edges in §3.1 is correct. The triangulation bound is also correct.

Fix: change “faces” to “edges” or “total cells,” retaining “triangles” for triangulations.

15. MINOR — Prop X1’s hypotheses disappear in the summaries.

Location: §0, lines 65–66; row 18; §3.2, lines 327–336.

The full proposition survives. The statement using only N>(√ℓ+1)² does not.

For N = 15 and ℓ = 7, the Hasse inequality excludes noncuspidal points, but there are eight rational cusps, not φ(15)/2 = 4. This also agrees with the genus-one point count.

The proof should refer to Y₁(N) for the fine moduli argument. Its use of m∈{p,q,N} also needs generalization if the theorem is genuinely stated for arbitrary squarefree N.

Fix: retain the prime-factor condition everywhere and give the cusp Galois action explicitly.

16. MINOR — KUM’s displayed dimension is correct; its concluding sentence is not.

Location: §5, lines 431–435.

The reviewed version actually uses Spec Z[1/(2N)]. For that scheme,

    dim H¹_et(−, μ₂) = ω(N)+2,

with the stated unit classes.

The next sentence says the dimension is ω(N), contradicting the display. It is the dimension minus two that recovers ω(N).

For Spec Z[1/N] with N odd, the fppf Kummer calculation gives ω(N)+1. The étale Kummer argument cannot simply be carried over without inverting 2.

Fix: preserve the distinction between schemes and topologies; require explicit unit representatives when discussing a basis as factoring data.

17. MINOR — Pairwise linking is not a Rédei triple symbol.

Location: §0, lines 87–89; row 30; OPEN-2.

The pairwise datum is the Legendre symbol. Rédei triple symbols belong to the higher-order story.

For p,q ≡1 mod 4, the narrow-class-group statement is correctly expressed as

    r₄(Cl⁺(Q(√pq))) = 1 if (p/q)=+1,
                       0 if (p/q)=−1.

OPEN-2 should also specify its reduction model. A polynomial-time extractor using only a single unknown bit at the same N can be simulated by trying both bit values and verifying the resulting factors. A meaningful oracle reduction must permit suitably specified changed inputs.

Fix: separate pairwise linking, the Rédei matrix, and triple symbols. Say “no polynomial-time algorithm known,” not “no algorithm from N alone.”

18. MINOR — Row 11 conflates an order class number, a field class number and genus counts.

Location: row 11.

The Bianchi cusp count uses the maximal-order class number of Q(√−N). At N = 35,

    h(−35)=2, whereas h(−140)=6.

Thus h(−4N) is not universally the Bianchi cusp count.

The assertion “2^{ω−1} genera” also misses the ramified factor 2. For N = 65, discriminant −260 has four genera, not two.

Fix: distinguish the order of discriminant −4N from the maximal order, and count prime discriminants in the genus formula.

19. MINOR — The class-number cost column is not the best known randomized bound.

Location: rows 5 and 11.

N^{1/5+o(1)} under ERH is a classical bound, not the best asymptotic randomized bound under that assumption. Subexponential class-group algorithms also compute the class number.

Fix: distinguish deterministic bounds from randomized bounds and include Hafner–McCurley and subsequent improvements. A modern primary reference is [Biasse–Erukulangara](https://doi.org/10.3934/amc.2021055).

20. MINOR — Normalization and ramification are conflated with listing prime factors.

Location: row 44; §6.

For squarefree d, the maximal order of Q(√d) is given by the familiar basis determined by d modulo 4. Computing that basis does not factor d. Likewise, returning a discriminant integer or a closed ramification subscheme differs from listing its constituent primes.

Fix: state Buchmann–Lenstra as a reduction between general computational problems. Separately identify which requested output lists ramified primes.

21. MAJOR — The plain-language verdict contains false statements and an incorrect tier assignment.

Location: §0; row 31.

Concrete repairs are needed:

- r₃(N)=12h(−4N) needs its congruence hypotheses. At N=15, r₃(15)=0 but 12h(−60)=24.
- “The same split holds for X₁(N)” is wrong: the prime-field count is already factoring-equivalent in Prop X1.
- N·vol(M_φ) is not AGG under the draft’s definition “a function of N modulo a fixed m.” It has infinitely many distinct values.
- The trace formulas establish dependence on specified residue data, not a theorem that growing collections of such data cannot factor.
- The explicit-complex size bounds do not rule out future compressed algorithms.
- “We can say exactly why in each case” and “none … is a live lead” are stronger than the results and remaining open questions justify.

Fix: state that no faster algorithm was obtained, with proved obstructions confined to the specified representations and models.

Checks

1. Prop X0-2 — PASS for the formula; FAIL for the accompanying recovery/separation claims.

The sign, outer factor 1/2, and exceptional weights h_w(−4)=1/2 and h_w(−3)=1/3 are correct. Define the f-sum explicitly by f²|D and D/f²≡0,1 mod 4.

For squarefree N, all X₀ cusps are rational. The prime-factor bound makes gcd(f,N)=1, so using D rather than D/f² in the Legendre factors is correct.

The formula proves a QR upper bound. I found no verified polynomial-time factoring method using the growing prime-field data, but the formula does not exclude one. E3’s stronger decoding assertion fails as above.

2. Prop X0-3 and extensions — PASS for the identities; FAIL for parts of their interpretation and scope.

A cleaner exact identity, for k≥2, is

    #X₀(N)(F_{ℓ^k})
      = ℓ^k+1 − Tr T_{ℓ^k} + ℓ Tr T_{ℓ^{k−2}}.

Here T₁=I. It follows directly from the two Frobenius roots and the Hecke recurrence.

For k=2 this gives the stated +ℓg. For odd k both Hecke indices are nonsquares. For even k both identity terms occur, giving coefficient (ℓ−1)/12. Retain the relevant level-prime bounds and discriminant-size dependence.

I independently enumerated 46 extension-field elliptic-curve counts, including degrees 3,4,5; all agreed. Eight additional Manin-symbol trace checks agreed. The supersingular interpretation needs issue 11’s correction.

3. Prop X1 — PASS with its complete hypotheses.

I counted cusps using their Frobenius action in 426 admissible cases with N<300. All gave φ(N)/2.

Independent curve checks gave:

    #X₁(11)(F₂), #X₁(11)(F₃), #X₁(11)(F₅) = 5,5,5;
    #X₁(13)(F₂), #X₁(13)(F₃), #X₁(13)(F₅) = 6,6,6.

For X₁(13), I enumerated the model
y²+(x³−x²−1)y=x²−x, including its two points at infinity. The [PARI documentation](https://pari.math.u-bordeaux.fr/pub/pari/manuals/2.17.1/users.pdf) identifies this model and its good reduction at 2.

4. Other propositions — FAIL overall.

NECK, CAT’s variable-trace arithmetic reduction, MF, MF′ and COV pass. The NECK/CAT linearizations were checked at four semiprimes; conic counts were directly enumerated at four levels.

AGG-H fails as stated.

CYC’s module quotient and fixed-coefficient periodicity pass. For knots, t−1 is invertible on the Alexander module, so A/ν_NA agrees with A/(t^N−1)A; in fact period m already suffices modulo ℓ. Its integer-growth and Möbius interpretations fail without the repairs above.

KUM’s ω+2 display passes for the scheme currently written.

The triangulation, edge-count and Farey-size bounds pass; the table’s arbitrary-CW face bound fails.

5. Class numbers, Fricke, Rédei and QR — FAIL overall.

The Fricke formula is correct for N>4 when h denotes primitive-form/order class numbers:

    #Fix(w_N)=h(−4N)+[N≡3 mod 4]h(−N).

The vague “classical corrections” are unnecessary in this stated range. Its FACT inference fails.

The Legendre/4-rank relation passes; the triple-symbol identification fails. Fixed small nonsquare-D residuosity remains an appropriate unresolved decision problem, distinct from producing square roots. Do not apply that unresolved-status wording to trivial square D or instances already decided by the Jacobi symbol.

6. OPEN-1 and OPEN-2 — FAIL as a pair of precise open problems.

OPEN-1 is solved literally and needs reformulation. OPEN-2 describes a legitimate unresolved polynomial-time question, but needs the symbol terminology and oracle model clarified. I found no verified new factor-extraction algorithm from these data.

7. Literature — FAIL for completeness and attribution.

Most named references are real and relevant. The major omission is prior art for the supersingular mechanism; verification details follow.

8. Plain-language verdict — FAIL.

The cautious conclusion “no faster algorithm obtained” is supported. The stronger exhaustive explanation, tier separation and dismissal of all leads are not.

The repository’s existing geometry tests passed: 7/7. They do not cover the counterexamples above. Reproducible scratch computations are in [referee_checks.py](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/geometry_2/referee_checks.py), with [output](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/geometry_2/referee_checks.json).

Literature

“Verified statement” below means I inspected the relevant accessible statement or formula, not every proof.

- Cohen 1977: bibliographic citation confirmed: “Trace des opérateurs de Hecke sur Γ₀(N),” Bordeaux seminar 1976–1977, Exp. 4, nine pages. I did not retrieve the original text. Cohen–Strömberg 2017, Thm 12.4.11 is the correct theorem number; its formula is reproduced explicitly in [Child, §4](https://arxiv.org/html/2101.05663v2). Verified through that reproduction.

- Merel 1994: verified the matrix formula, specifically Proposition 20 in [Universal Fourier expansions of modular forms](https://wstein.org/edu/Fall2003/252/references/merel-universal/Merel_Universal_Fourier_expansions_of_modular_forms.pdf), LNM 1585, pp. 59–94. The earlier [1991 Γ₀(N) paper](https://numdam.org/articles/10.5802/aif.1264/) is also relevant; these are separate references.

- Kulkarni 1991: bibliographic record verified, American Journal of Mathematics 113(6), 1053–1133. I checked the applicable Farey results through Kurth–Long, not the entire original paper.

- Kurth–Long: verified §3.4, including μ=3n+e₃, in [the paper](https://arxiv.org/html/0710.1835v1). The preprint is 2007; publication is Advances in Algebra and Combinatorics, 2008, pp. 225–242.

- Ogg 1974: verified §2’s Fricke formula and §3, Theorem 3’s supersingular argument in [Hyperelliptic modular curves](https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.1789.pdf). Ogg explicitly uses E:y²+y=x³, scalar Frobenius −2 over F₄, all cyclic subgroups being rational, and the ψ(N)/12 contribution. The geometric mechanism is established prior art. I did not locate an earlier statement of the draft’s exact factoring reduction.

- Eichler mass/class-number formulas are additional prior art for the supersingular count. [Voight’s mass-formula chapter](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-56694-4_25) gives mass φ(D)ψ(M)/12 for definite Eichler orders over Q; the class-number formula adds embedding corrections. This is the appropriate framework for separating mass from unweighted point counts.

- X₁ cusp-only counts: I verified standard cusp descriptions and small explicit models, including the cusp discussion in [Derickx’s thesis](https://www.maartenderickx.nl/articles/Proefschrift.pdf). The Hasse-bound exclusion of Y₁ points is classical. I did not locate a published theorem with exactly the draft’s general hypotheses and factoring conclusion; that negative search is not a novelty certificate.

- Rédei 1934: bibliography confirmed for the 4-rank papers, including Rédei–Reichardt, J. reine angew. Math. 170, 69–74, and Rédei, volume 171, 55–60. The triple symbol belongs to Rédei’s 1939 work. [Stevenhagen 2022](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004121000335) explains its corrected definition and its role in 8-ranks.

- Morishita 2012: [book and chapter subjects verified](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4471-2158-9). Chapter 4 concerns linking numbers and Legendre symbols; chapter 8 concerns Milnor invariants and multiple residue symbols. These support the distinctions above.

- Neumann–Zagier 1985: bibliography verified: [Volumes of hyperbolic three-manifolds](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0040938385900047), Topology 24(3), 307–332. I did not complete a full-text deformation-theorem audit. The draft should restrict its analytic statement to the appropriate neighborhood of the complete structure and sufficiently long hyperbolic fillings.

- Silver–Williams 2002: verified the growth statement and its qualifications in [Mahler measure, links and homology growth](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0040-9383(01)00014-3), Topology 41(5), 979–991.

- Buchmann–Lenstra 1994: verified Theorem 1.3 in [Approximatting rings of integers in number fields](https://www.numdam.org/item/JTNB_1994__6_2_221_0.pdf), JTNB 6(2), 221–260. It concerns the general maximal-order problem and the largest squarefree divisor, not listing factors of a known squarefree quadratic discriminant.

- Bach–Miller–Shallit 1986: verified the divisor-sum factoring result in [Sums of divisors, perfect numbers and factoring](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~glmiller/Publications/Papers/BMS86.pdf), SIAM J. Comput. 15(4), 1143–1154.

- Gu–Martin 2019: verified Theorem 10 in [the authors’ text](https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/papers/downloads/FTAACMFAR.pdf). It uses two automorphic-representation counts and a newform count. The draft’s qualification of this theorem is correct.

- Shallit–Shamir 1985: verified metadata and the authors’ abstract for [Number-theoretic functions which are equivalent to number of divisors](https://weizmann.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/number-theoretic-functions-which-are-equivalent-to-number-of-divi/), IPL 20(3), 151–153. This is about divisor-count oracles; it is not a general factoring-equivalence theorem.

- Necklace/periodic-orbit factoring prior art: I did not locate a published equivalence for these exact variable-parameter modular oracles. The underlying Burnside and Möbius identities are classical. Nothing verified in this search supplies the missing fixed-trace theorem.

Anything missed

The taxonomy omits ordinary inexpensive non-aggregate features: N·vol(M_φ) is already one example in the draft. Such features need not reveal unavailable factor information, but their existence defeats an exhaustive “aggregate/QR/FACT” classification without further definitions.

The worthwhile distinction is between a known algorithm, an oracle reduction, an output-size obstruction for a specified representation, and an unresolved computational problem. The draft repeatedly moves between these. Keeping those distinctions explicit would preserve its useful core without claiming a general barrier to geometric factoring methods.